{
  "summary": "This study investigates how dual twisted interfaces in CVD-grown trilayer MoS2 affect second harmonic generation (SHG). By fabricating nine distinct configurations, researchers analyzed Raman and photoluminescence data to show stacking-dependent phonon and excitonic responses. Atomic-resolution techniques confirmed dual twisted interfaces and a supermoiré lattice. The study developed a framework linking dual interfaces to SHG intensity and polarization, establishing TT-MoS2 as a tunable nonlinear optical platform.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Dual twisted interfaces in trilayer MoS2 create stacking-dependent phonon and excitonic responses",
    "Atomic-resolution techniques confirmed dual twisted interfaces and a supermoiré lattice structure",
    "A framework linking dual interfaces to SHG intensity and polarization was developed for engineering nonlinear optical responses"
  ],
  "study_type": "CVD-grown twisted trilayer MoS2 configurations",
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a foundational framework for engineering multi-interface twisted coupling to tune nonlinear optical responses in van der Waals materials."
}

{
  "summary": "This study integrated single-cell epigenomic data from 2.5 million adult mouse brain nuclei to construct a comprehensive atlas. It combined four histone modifications, transcriptomes, chromatin accessibility, DNA methylation, and 3D genome organization. The atlas identified 100+ cell subclasses and defined active, primed, and repressive chromatin states across 81% of the genome. Active states with combinatorial histone marks better predict functional enhancers than chromatin accessibility alone, while H3K27me3 and H3K9me3 mark distinct repression mechanisms. This resource enables deep learning models for predicting cell-type-specific epigenomic features and gene expression from DNA sequences.",
  "key_findings": [
    "The atlas spans over 100 brain cell subclasses using integrated epigenomic data from 2.5 million nuclei.",
    "Active chromatin states defined by combinatorial histone modifications better predict functional enhancers than chromatin accessibility alone.",
    "H3K27me3- and H3K9me3-marked chromatin states delineate distinct modes of cell-type-specific gene repression."
  ],
  "study_type": "Single-cell epigenomic profiling",
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a unified framework for annotating the regulatory genome of the adult mouse brain, enabling precise interpretation of non-coding disease risk variants through cell-type-specific epigenomic features."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates mechanisms of allergic relapse in the skin by identifying a pathogenic CXCR6+ subset of skin-resident memory CD4+ T cells that persist despite biologic treatment in human atopic dermatitis. The approach involved characterizing T cell subsets in patients with atopic dermatitis. The findings indicate these cells contribute to disease relapse post-treatment.",
  "key_findings": [
    "CXCR6+ skin-resident memory CD4+ T cells persist after biologic therapy in atopic dermatitis",
    "These cells are pathogenic and drive skin allergic relapse",
    "The subset remains present despite treatment, suggesting a mechanism for relapse"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This identifies a persistent pathogenic T cell subset driving skin allergic relapse despite treatment, advancing understanding of allergic disease mechanisms."
}

{
  "summary": "This study elucidates the structural basis of New World hantavirus entry via protocadherin-1 (PCDH1) using near-atomic resolution cryo-EM. It reveals how PCDH1 asymmetrically binds a deep pocket in the viral spike, explains receptor specificity between New and Old World hantaviruses, and demonstrates antibody mechanisms to block this interaction for therapeutic design.",
  "key_findings": [
    "PCDH1 binds a deep pocket formed by four Gn protomers in the hantavirus spike",
    "Three Gn loops determine receptor specificity, transferring binding from New to Old World hantaviruses",
    "Neutralizing antibodies block PCDH1 recognition via steric hindrance or site occlusion"
  ],
  "study_type": "cryo-EM",
  "scientific_significance": "This work defines the structural mechanism of hantavirus-receptor recognition, revealing how viral spikes conceal binding sites and informing antibody-based therapeutic strategies against hantavirus infections."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates whether cellular agriculture technologies, including precision fermentation and cultivated meat, can achieve cost-effective, scalable production for widespread adoption. It assesses enabling technologies, food functionality, and economic viability, identifying that manufacturing efficiency surpasses technological innovation as the critical barrier to commercial success. The research emphasizes functional improvements over direct food replication for broader impact.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Precision fermentation benefits most from functional enhancements in taste, nutrition, and health rather than direct food imitation",
    "Cultivated meat adoption will initially focus on hybrid products that capture specific attributes without full tissue mimicry costs",
    "Manufacturing scalability and affordability are more critical for commercial adoption than ongoing biological technology development"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This work shifts the focus from technological innovation to practical manufacturing strategies, providing a framework for overcoming economic and functional barriers to sustainable cellular agriculture implementation."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how protein kinase A (PKA) signaling regulates group 3 innate lymphoid cell (ILC3) homeostasis and its role in gut inflammation. Using a single-cell atlas, researchers found that prostaglandin activates PKA to promote tissue-protective functions, while interleukin-23 (IL-23) suppresses PKA inhibitors, leading to ILC3 depletion and increased susceptibility to enteric infections. The study demonstrates that PRKAR1A maintains ILC3 homeostasis in adults, and IL-23 overproduction triggers PKA-driven inflammation, with human intestinal ILC3s expressing PRKAR1A that is downregulated in inflammatory bowel disease.",
  "key_findings": [
    "IL-23 suppresses PKA inhibitors in ILC3s, reducing their numbers and increasing infection susceptibility",
    "PRKAR1A is essential for maintaining ILC3 homeostasis in adult mice, with early life not requiring it",
    "IL-23 overproduction in ILC3s increases PKA signaling, causing gut inflammation and downregulating PRKAR1A"
  ],
  "study_type": "Single-cell atlas analysis in mice and human intestinal tissue",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes PKA signaling as a critical regulator of ILC3-mediated gut homeostasis, revealing how IL-23 disrupts this balance to drive inflammation through PRKAR1A modulation."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates a 3D-printed multi-principal-element alloy (MPEA) designed to maintain strength-ductility across temperatures via an intrinsic cellular network formed during laser additive manufacturing. The alloy features a self-assembled body-centred cubic/L21 precipitate structure that enables slip transfer and back-stress hardening across specific interfaces. This mechanism stabilizes deformation from 77 to 873 K, achieving yield strengths above 1 GPa and uniform elongation of ~20% without post-processing.",
  "key_findings": [
    "A (CrFeCo)48Ni34Al7Ti5V5Ta1 MPEA with a self-assembled body-centred cubic/L21 precipitate cellular network formed intrinsically during LAM",
    "Slip transfer and back-stress hardening across Kurdjumov-Sachs and Nishiyama-Wassermann interfaces stabilize deformation from 77 to 873 K",
    "The alloy maintains yield strength >1 GPa and uniform elongation ~20% over a wide temperature range without post-treatment"
  ],
  "study_type": "Laser additive manufacturing (LAM) of multi-principal-element alloys",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a process-intrinsic pathway for designing temperature-stable, high-strength alloys through interface-mediated plasticity mechanisms in additive manufacturing."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates language models' ability to perform sequential diagnosis by simulating physician decision-making. The AI system iteratively revised differential diagnoses and selected cost-effective tests using a benchmark. It achieved 83.2% accuracy on NEJM cases and 8.03% on MIMIC-IV-ED admissions, significantly outperforming generalist physicians. The findings suggest language models could enhance diagnostic accuracy while reducing costs in clinical settings.",
  "key_findings": [
    "The AI system achieved 83.2% accuracy on NEJM clinicopathological conference cases",
    "The system attained 80.3% accuracy on MIMIC-IV-ED emergency department admissions",
    "The model outperformed generalist physicians by 47.1 percentage points in NEJM cases and 34.5 points in MIMIC-IV-ED admissions"
  ],
  "study_type": "Interactive benchmark with AI system simulating physicians",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates language models can effectively support sequential clinical diagnosis with higher accuracy and lower costs compared to generalist physicians, advancing AI applications in diagnostic medicine."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the structural basis of IL-36R signaling by IL-36γ and IL-37 using cryo-EM. It reveals IL-36γ binds IL-36R with low affinity, enabling high-affinity IL-1RAcP recruitment, while IL-37 binds with high affinity but weakly recruits IL-1RAcP. Functional assays confirm both cytokines activate proinflammatory pathways, with IL-36γ showing higher potency. The findings provide a structural blueprint for understanding IL-36R activation mechanisms.",
  "key_findings": [
    "IL-36γ binds IL-36R with low affinity, enabling high-affinity IL-1RAcP recruitment",
    "IL-37 binds IL-36R with high affinity but weakly recruits IL-1RAcP",
    "IL-36γ exhibits markedly higher signaling potency than IL-37"
  ],
  "study_type": "Cryo-electron microscopy",
  "scientific_significance": "This structural analysis elucidates the molecular mechanisms underlying cytokine-specific activation of IL-36R, advancing understanding of inflammatory signaling pathways and therapeutic targeting strategies."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates closed nanopores as internal stress-regulation units to mitigate mechanical instability in silicon-carbon anodes for lithium-ion batteries. By engineering heteroatom-doped carbon hosts with abundant closed pores, the material redirects lithiation-induced stress inward, suppressing electrode swelling. The nitrogen-doped composite achieves 1756.5 mAh g-1 reversible capacity, 90.2% initial Coulombic efficiency, and 868.4 mAh g-1 at 3 C, while reducing thickness expansion from 38.4% to 13.1% after 50 cycles. The design demonstrates scalability for practical battery applications.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Closed nanopores in heteroatom-doped carbon hosts redirect lithiation-induced stress inward, suppressing electrode swelling",
    "Nitrogen-doped silicon-carbon composite achieves 1756.5 mAh g-1 reversible capacity and 868.4 mAh g-1 at 3 C",
    "The design reduces thickness expansion from 38.4% to 13.1% after 50 cycles and enables scalable production for 2 Ah NCM811 pouch cells"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro engineering of heteroatom-doped carbon hosts with closed nanopores",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes closed-pore stress regulation as a scalable structural strategy for practical low-expansion silicon-carbon anodes, addressing critical mechanical and electrochemical instability challenges in high-energy lithium-ion batteries."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how conventional type 1 dendritic cells (cDC1s) mediate next-generation checkpoint blockade immunotherapy. The authors demonstrate that mature cDC1s assemble costimulatory signals critical for CD8⁺ T cell fate determination and therapeutic efficacy. Their findings reveal cDC1s as central effectors in this immunotherapeutic approach.",
  "key_findings": [
    "cDC1s assemble costimulatory signals that determine CD8⁺ T cell fate",
    "cDC1s are essential for next-generation checkpoint blockade therapeutic efficacy",
    "cDC1s act as key orchestrators of antitumor immunity in immunotherapy"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "The identification of cDC1s as central effectors in next-generation checkpoint blockade immunotherapy provides mechanistic insight into how dendritic cells orchestrate antitumor immunity and CD8⁺ T cell responses, advancing understanding of immunotherapy mechanisms."
  }

{
  "summary": "This study investigated salt marsh resilience during extreme storms using a wave flume experiment with intact marshes exposed to up to 2 m significant wave heights. Results indicate marsh substrates remain stable despite wave exposure, while vegetation progressively degrades, reducing wave attenuation. Attenuation exceeds 10% at low-energy coasts (≤1 m wave height) but remains below 10% at high-energy coasts (>1 m wave height).",
  "key_findings": [
    "Marsh substrates persist with negligible erosion under cumulative wave exposure up to 2 m significant wave height",
    "Vegetation degradation progressively reduces wave attenuation during prolonged wave exposure",
    "Wave attenuation by marsh vegetation exceeds 10% at low-energy coasts (≤1 m wave height) but remains below 10% at high-energy coasts (>1 m wave height)"
  ],
  "study_type": "Wave flume experiment",
  "scientific_significance": "The study provides critical evidence on salt marsh resilience thresholds under extreme wave events, clarifying the conditions where vegetation-driven wave attenuation remains effective for coastal protection."
}

{
  "summary": "This study evaluates the deployability of 77 biotechnologies across eight agricultural domains using a Balanced Readiness Level framework assessing technological, market, regulatory, acceptance, and organizational factors. It identifies market viability and social acceptance as primary constraints over technological readiness. High-readiness technologies exhibit 'drop-in', contained, transient, and natural perceptions, suggesting strategies like improved GMO containment to enhance deployability for environmentally resilient agriculture.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Market viability and social acceptance are primary constraints for biotechnology deployability",
    "High-readiness technologies are characterized by 'drop-in', contained, transient, and natural perceptions",
    "Strategies to improve deployability include addressing GMO containment and scaling biotechnologies for environmental resilience"
  ],
  "study_type": "Balanced Read: 77 biotechnologies across eight agricultural domains",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a systematic framework for assessing biotechnology deployability, highlighting the critical role of market and social factors in enabling environmentally resilient agricultural systems through scalable biological solutions."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates engineering dendritic cells with chimeric antigen receptors (CARs) using CLEC9A and DEC205 intracellular domains to enhance antitumor immunity. The approach involves creating CAR-DCs that improve antigen uptake, cross-presentation, and T cell activation within solid tumors. Results show CAR-DCs suppress tumor growth and expand tumor-reactive T cells in preclinical models, demonstrating their potential as scalable immunotherapeutic platforms.",
  "key_findings": [
    "CAR-DCs using CLEC9A or DEC205 intracellular domains enhance antigen uptake and cross-presentation",
    "CAR-DCs trigger SYK phosphorylation and downstream signaling pathways",
    "CAR-DC therapy suppresses tumor growth and expands tumor-reactive T cells in mouse models"
  ],
  "study_type": "Preclinical mouse models",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes CAR-DCs as a clinically translatable platform for solid tumor immunotherapy by reprogramming dendritic cell function to overcome tumor microenvironment constraints."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates adapt-ring networks (ARNs), synthetic polymer systems that autonomously remodel and strengthen under mechanical stress. ARNs integrate covalent polyethylene glycol networks with reconfigurable α-cyclodextrin domains. Mechanical training enhances mechanical properties by up to an order of magnitude, with full reversibility via solvent treatment. The findings enable gradient materials and strain-controlled self-strengthening mechanisms.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Mechanical training increases Young's modulus, tensile strength, fracture energy, and fatigue threshold by up to an order of magnitude",
    "Full reversibility of mechanical properties through solvent-induced reformation of crystalline domains",
    "Strain-controlled out-of-equilibrium growth via threading and crystallization of external α-cyclodextrins"
  ],
  "study_type": "Synthetic polymer network",
  "scientific_significance": "ARNs provide a general design principle for bio-inspired soft materials that autonomously adapt, remember mechanical history, and self-strengthen without equilibrium constraints."
}

{
  "summary": "This study used scanning tunneling microscopy to investigate Hofstad: states in bilayer graphene on hexagonal boron nitride. It visualized interaction-induced symmetry breaking Chern insulators, revealing phases with doubled, tripled, or quadrupled moiré unit cells at fractional fillings. The research also observed quantum melting via topological defects and phase competition in distinct Chern states.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Direct visualization of interaction-driven symmetry breaking Chern insulators in moiré quantum materials",
    "Formation of phases with doubled, tripled, or quadrupled moiré unit cells at fractional Hofstadter band fillings",
    "Observation of quantum melting through topological defect proliferation and phase competition in Chern states"
  ],
  "study_type": "Scanning tunneling microscopy",
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides direct experimental evidence of interaction-induced symmetry breaking in topological quantum materials, advancing understanding of how electron-electron interactions shape moiré band topology and phase transitions."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how aberrant bone marrow-derived myelopoiesis contributes to neuroinflammation in chronic traumatic brain injury. Using molecular and cellular analyses, it found that myeloid cells excessively proliferate in injured brains, exacerbating inflammation. The results suggest that targeting myeloid cell activity could mitigate neuroinflammation in chronic TBI.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Aberrant bone marrow-derived myelopoiesis drives neuroinflammation in chronic traumatic brain injury",
    "Myeloid cells excessively proliferate in injured brains",
    "Targeting myel:oid cell activity may reduce neuroinflammation in chronic TBI"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "The findings establish a causal link between dysregulated myelopoiesis and neuroinflammation in chronic traumatic brain injury, providing a potential therapeutic target for managing persistent inflammatory responses post-injury."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates decoupling protection and doping in Zn single-atom catalysts to enhance oxygen reduction reaction (OR. The sacrificial etching strategy uses Bi(NO3)3 for coordination preservation and NaCl for Cl doping, creating asymmetric Zn centers with improved ORR performance. The catalyst achieves a half-wave potential of 0.916 V and peak power density of 258 mW cm-2, exceeding commercial Pt/C.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Bi(NO3)3-mediated protection preserves ZnN4 coordination during etching",
    "NaCl-driven Cl doping imparts electron-withdrawing effects to modulate electronic structure",
    "Asymmetric Zn single atoms achieve record ORR half-wave potential of 0.916 V"
  ],
  "study_type": "Sacrificial etching strategy with in situ spectroscopic analysis",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a precise method for engineering single-atom metal centers through decoupled protection and doping, advancing non-precious metal electrocatalyst design for oxygen reduction reactions."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how mitochondrial ATP dynamics affect CD8+ T cell chromatin remodeling and function. Using D-α-hydroxybutyrate (DAHB) as a metabolic regulator, it shows DAHB enhances phosphocreatine production, which increases chromatin accessibility and improves tumor-limiting CD8+ T cell effector responses. The findings suggest metabolic pathways critically influence T cell epigenetic regulation for anti-tumor immunity.",
  "key_findings": [
    "DAHB boosts phosphoc:reatine production",
    "Enhanced chromatin accessibility in CD8+ T cells",
    "Improved tumor-limiting effector function"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo models",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a metabolic mechanism linking phosphocreatine to chromatin remodeling in T cells, providing new insights into how energy metabolism regulates immune cell epigenetics for anti-tumor responses."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how proteostatic damage in mitochondria triggers inter-organellar quality control. Using quantitative proteomics and correlative light-electron microscopy, researchers identified stress-induced mitochondrial protein aggregates (SIMPAs) that activate GRP75-dependent signaling at mitochondria-endoplasmic reticulum contact sites. The findings demonstrate SIMPAs coordinate targeted clearance of damaged proteins via endoplasmic reticulum unfolded protein response pathways, preserving mitochondrial integrity under proteotoxic stress.",
  "key_findings": [
    "SIMPAs form in response to mitochondrial proteostatic stress and activate GRP75-dependent quality control mechanisms",
    "GRP75 coordinates SIMPA biogenesis and focal activation of endoplasmic reticulum unfolded protein response pathways",
    "GRP75-dependent SIMPA signaling attenuates mitochondrial stress and maintains mitochondrial integrity in cellular and liver disease models"
  ],
  "study_type": "Quantitative proteomics and correlative light-electron microscopy",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes SIMPAs as a central protein condensate hub for inter-organellar communication in mitochondrial quality control, providing a mechanism for stress signal propagation across organelles without disrupting mitochondrial networks."
}

{
  "summary": "This review addresses the challenge of modeling human biology by highlighting the limitations of conventional in vitro and animal models at critical interfaces where matrix, immune, and systemic signals interact. It proposes integrating engineered materials, organoids, microphysiological systems, and computational models to create more comprehensive human-relevant systems while acknowledging the current gap in closed-loop feedback mechanisms.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Engineered materials can control local environmental cues such as matrix composition and stiffness",
    "Organoids and microphysiological systems require functional benchmarking and vascularization for relevance",
    "Computational models aid in organizing data and improving prediction but lack closed-loop integration"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This work advances the field by establishing a framework for integrating diverse model systems to better capture complex human biological interfaces, emphasizing the necessity of balancing model complexity with the specific biological questions being addressed."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how poetry enhances ecological understanding and motivation for planetary conservation. Using a conceptual framework, it examines the role of metaphor in fostering environmental awareness. The research found that poetic language improves comprehension of climate challenges and increases engagement with sustainability actions compared to technical explanations.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Poetic language improves comprehension of climate challenges",
    "Poetry increases engagement with sustainability actions",
    "Metaphors enhance ecological understanding for planetary conservation"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates that metaphorical communication can bridge the gap between technical environmental science and public motivation for conservation efforts."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates in vivo cell engineering for immune cell programming within patients, bypassing ex vivo cell manipulation. It evaluates delivery methods like lipid nanoparticles and viral vectors to target specific immune cells, aiming to enhance precision in vector delivery, payload expression, and safety. Results indicate progress in targeting ligands and vector design improving feasibility for oncology and autoimmunity applications.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Advancements in targeting ligands and vector design improve precision for immune cell engineering",
    "Early clinical studies demonstrate feasibility in oncology and auto:immunity",
    "Safety concerns including immunogenicity and off-target effects remain critical challenges"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This work advances the field by demonstrating that in vivo immune cell engineering can be technically feasible for specific diseases, potentially expanding cell therapies to previously untreatable conditions through direct body-based programming."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates NET-driven osteoblast mitochondrial dysfunction in osteomyelitis. Using murine models and gallium-selenium nanoparticles (GaSex), researchers demonstrated that NET accumulation impairs osteogenesis via PINK1/Parkin-mediated mitophagy disruption. GaSex redirected neutrophils from NET formation to phagocytosis, preserving osteoblast function and accelerating bone repair in infected bone defects.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Excessive neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs) cause osteoblast apoptosis and impaired osteogenesis in osteomyelitis",
    "PINK1/Parkin-dependent mitophagy disruption by NETs drives osteoblast mitochondrial injury",
    "Gallium-selenium nanoparticles (GaSex) reduce NET formation, enhance neutrophil phagocytosis, and improve bone repair"
  ],
  "study_type": "Murine models",
  "scientific_significance": "The findings establish NET-mediated mitochondrial dysfunction as a critical barrier to bone repair in osteomyelitis and provide a redox-regulating strategy to overcome this barrier."
}
